   249	
   250	## 4. The wall, precisely
   251	
   252	> **⚠ BOUNDED (2026-06-07, see §0/§G/§7):** this §4 "collapse wins on every axis"
   253	> analysis was computed on the **pre-faithful-env** regime whose base channel
   254	> lacked off-axis antenna gain (beams channel-identical) — a confounded setup. On
   255	> the faithful QoS-live env (SDD-04) the env rewards **spread** (`B/N_b` + r3) and
   256	> collapse is reward-**SUBOPTIMAL but unreached**. Read §4 as historical provenance,
   257	> not the current established verdict. The wording below is NOT rewritten (the §4
   258	> analysis is codex-gated); the §0 + §G/§7 entries carry the corrected, bounded
   259	> finding.
   260	
   261	A single active beam serving all users ("collapse") wins on every axis the reward
   262	cares about:
   263	
   264	- **F1 — Energy efficiency.** EE = throughput / power. Fewer active beams = less
   265	  total power. Collapse (1 beam) minimizes the denominator → **collapse maximizes
   266	  EE.**
   267	- **F2 — Interference.** Inter-beam interference exists only *between* active
   268	  beams. Collapse = 1 active beam = **zero interference**. Spreading lights
   269	  co-channel beams that interfere → **interference punishes spread, never
   270	  collapse.**
   271	- **F3 — Bandwidth.** Per-beam bandwidth divides among that beam's users (`B/N`),
   272	  so total throughput on one beam = `B·log2(1+SNR)` regardless of how many users
   273	  pile on. Piling users onto one beam loses nothing in sum-rate.
   274	
   275	On top of the reward landscape, the **execution structure** guarantees collapse
   276	even when it isn't forced: MODQN uses **shared Q-heads**, and at deployment **each
   277	user independently takes `argmax`** over the same Q. Similar users → same argmax →
   278	**all users choose the same beam** (`ASSUME-MODQN-REP-007`). Training-time tricks
   279	get "argmax'd away" at deployment unless they change *which beam each user picks
   280	relative to others*.
   281	
   282	**Three traps** look like fixes but are not (each matches a documented failure):
   283	
   284	- **Trap A — interference alone reinforces collapse.** Adding co-channel
   285	  interference only taxes spread (collapse is `I=0`). Makes collapse *more*
   286	  attractive, not less.
   287	- **Trap B — a hard capacity cap forces a split, but the split is
   288	  geometry-deterministic.** With identical users a cap forces exactly *L* per
   289	  beam; any admission rule achieves it → the learned coordination layer is
   290	  **redundant**.
   291	- **Trap C — load-divided bandwidth is already our collapse mode.** `(B/N)·log2`
   292	  is exactly the structure the current env has and which tie-collapsed.
   293	
   294	**To break the wall you need BOTH, and neither has been achieved:**
   295	1. an execution structure where a user's choice **differentiates** from others
   296	   (the coordination decoder attempted this), AND
   297	2. a regime where **spread is genuinely reward-better than collapse** (the HOBS
   298	   audit, the 63→32-paper survey, and the two-coupling spike all failed to find
   299	   one).
   300	
