/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/C1C2-ENCODER-REPAIR-2026-09-10.md:69:The admissible C1 repair buys **no measured predictive power**. Its value is exactly `1.0` at all 30 corpus anchors because every current base configuration reaches the RF cap, so standardization removes it. This negative result is not evidence that the feature is wrongly chosen for identifiability; it shows that breaking one contract witness does not supply variation useful on this corpus. C2 is unchanged because no admissible separator exists.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/C1C2-ENCODER-REPAIR-2026-09-10.md:77:C1 cannot presently be expected to raise pooled energy efficiency. Its Q-only held-out EV is worse than the mean predictor, and the smallest admissible collision-breaking feature is constant on this sample and changes nothing. The exact C1 label is structurally sensitive to coupled whole-network effects that the action-local row still does not represent. A training run would not repair missing information and is not authorized by this diagnostic.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/C1C2-ENCODER-REPAIR-2026-09-10.md:79:C2 also cannot presently be expected to raise pooled energy efficiency. Although Q2's held-out EV is positive (`0.2107`), its stored survival semantics are wrong on 822/1,784 two-anchor rows, and the exact target varies with future carrier-transition context absent from admissible deployment inputs. Correcting offline flags by copying coupled forecast outcomes would be leakage, not a deployable encoder repair. Before any efficacy claim, the focal-survival target must be made a function of admissible decision-time information (or the per-user C2 head design reconsidered), then evaluated on a fresh untouched split. No pooled-EE conclusion follows here.
