/home/sat/mcrl-v025-oracle-ws/FASTEST-PATH-Codex-2026-09-10.md:7:2. **The set route is not restricted to cross-beam interference.** A per-beam rule already sees an entire beam's users; a sum of per-user terms need not capture shared activation costs, occupancy thresholds, or simultaneous moves. Therefore the fast look's 2.1% does **not** bound this residual. Today's target-design report finds large residual corrections, including cancellation of exaggerated singleton sums. That establishes neither useful decisions nor learnability, but it defeats the assertion that the residual is intrinsically tiny. Joint association/control is a plausible arrangement to test, not a promised success.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-oracle-ws/FASTEST-PATH-Codex-2026-09-10.md:9:3. **Dispatch `ORACLE-RESIDUAL-TOGGLE` now: no training, two workers, eight preselected cached anchors under corrected physics.** Before the exact-search prefix, build one bounded candidate catalogue containing the incumbent, singleton moves, shared-beam bundles/evacuations, and coarse joint power/mode alternatives. Compare **exact summed per-user terms** with **those identical terms plus the exact set residual**; hold candidate coverage, observations, service constraints, and tie-breaking fixed. Use objective-aligned 48-boundary evaluation and pooled totals, with a common ratio optimization across anchors—not the old fixed-price snapshot. Report demand-capped bits/joule alongside capacity, and save controls and per-user delivery records.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-oracle-ws/FASTEST-PATH-Codex-2026-09-10.md:11:   The decisive number is the pooled-efficiency gain from enabling the residual, **not its prediction error or magnitude**. Use 10% as an explicit planning screen for “substantial.” A large gain establishes an opportunity worth learning; a small gain stops this candidate design, not every conceivable arrangement. No small bounded search can honestly prove universal impossibility. If it passes, run the smallest paired **FULL versus each of the three neutral-source DROP arms**, retaining and training every head, on held-out worlds. All three must deliver substantial gains; the oracle screen alone cannot establish that.
