/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/SET-LEVEL-DECODER-REVIVAL-2026-09-10.md:244:**VERIFIED adjacent bound, not an estimate.** The 22-anchor C3 coalition build evaluated 4,552 multi-user rows—mean 206.9 per anchor—in one fresh dense boundary-0 batch per anchor. That joint call cost `0.865742–20.973042 s`, mean `4.614247 s`; the timer excludes tape construction, catalogue/singleton work, context, serialization, and verification. It shows that added set evaluation is material and highly variable. Because its panel and timer differ from FULL20, it must not be added to today's median or called the expected S0 cost.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/SET-LEVEL-DECODER-REVIVAL-2026-09-10.md:246:**INFERRED engineering target.** Cache reuse/batching is the decisive seam: the current catalogue already pays to evaluate BASE and the singleton neighbourhood. A port that throws those outcomes away recreates precisely the repeated nominal-physics cost that dominates the current path. Whether a correct integrated S0 can meet V0.25's 10 s coordinator budget can only be answered by timing the adapter under the declared worker count and cancellation path.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/SET-LEVEL-DECODER-REVIVAL-2026-09-10.md:262:- `A0`: repaired masked `argmax(Q1+Q2)` complete proposal;
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/SET-LEVEL-DECODER-REVIVAL-2026-09-10.md:277:**Computational cost specification, not a time estimate.** Per anchor: one existing repaired-Q1/Q2 proposal; one sealed catalogue build with singleton nominal outcomes retained; one added dense boundary-0 batch for the multi-user rows; exact score/guard/tie; and one realised dense full-48 batch containing the two selected profiles. Across 20 anchors that is 20 catalogue builds, 20 added multi-user batches, and 40 committed-profile endpoint evaluations. The adjacent 22-anchor inventory suggests about 207 multi-user rows per anchor, but the run must record the actual FULL20 count rather than assume it. No C3 training is required. Execute only under `nice -n 15`, all BLAS thread variables set to 1, one Python process (never more than two), enforced peak RSS below 4 GB, and without overlapping or disturbing the live jobs.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/SET-LEVEL-DECODER-REVIVAL-2026-09-10.md:283:- **Verified by artefact/code:** both V0.23 numerical results and their BASE references; S0's realised-membership catalogue leak; C3-S's repaired nominal isolation; full-buffer numerators; panels and missing physical dates; no decoder learner; absent confirmatory result; old and current timing decompositions; V0.25 evaluator disagreement and cache defect; incomplete runtime prerequisites.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/BASE-COLLAPSE-DIAGNOSIS-2026-09-10.md:9:The primary learned result is the newer q1-v2 design run. For every available exact epoch-500 checkpoint, I replayed the FULL-arm heads on every action row in the 22 launch-receipt anchors and selected each user's stable masked `argmax(Q1 + Q2)`. This is the raw independent `a0`, before joint-conflict repair: the implementation loops over users and sums the two head scores independently ([deployment.py:101](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/deployment.py:101), [deployment.py:112](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/deployment.py:112)); repair is a later operation ([deployment.py:125](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/deployment.py:125)). Physical beam identity means `(NORAD ID, beam-chain ID)`, not an action-table slot.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/BASE-COLLAPSE-DIAGNOSIS-2026-09-10.md:67:There is no reinforcement-learning reward in this learner. It performs fixed-target pairwise regression; the code explicitly states that no reward, next state, target network, discount, or bootstrap exists ([learner.py:278](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/learner.py:278)). The nonlinear heads fit candidate-minus-reference targets ([learner.py:779](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/learner.py:779)). Loss values therefore provide no evidence here about concentration or EE.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/LIT-PHYSICS-CONVENTIONS-2026-09-10.md:109:The failure is one of *ordering*, and it is repairable by moving the tenth-percentile multiplier upstream of the power solve. That change converts the construction into Convention B at ~90 % availability, which is defensible.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/LIT-PHYSICS-CONVENTIONS-2026-09-10.md:162:- **Degraded and counted, but counted *visibly*.** Li et al. 2026's `min(C, D)` (§III-B) is degradation-with-service, and it is paired with an explicitly reported satisfaction rate (below).
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/LIT-PHYSICS-CONVENTIONS-2026-09-10.md:174:Also relevant: Zhang, Fu & Yang's headline rate metric is *not* pooled. §II-C eq. (10): `R_ave(t) = Σ R^m_n,i(t) / Σ u^m_n,i(t)` — sum of rates divided by the **number of connected users**, a per-user average. Combined with `ξ(t)`, the pair distinguishes "how well are served users doing" from "how many users are failing."
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-probe-ws/LIT-PHYSICS-CONVENTIONS-2026-09-10.md:230:- **Also present, and material to the project's claims.** (i) **Bandwidth shared equally among the users of a beam**: `R_n,m,k = (1 − C_n/T_f)(W_n,m / U_n,m) log₂(1 + γ)`, §II-B eq. (9), "We allocate the bandwidth equally among the users served by the same beam to ensure fairness" — this makes the achievable rate an explicit function of beam occupancy `U_n,m`. (ii) **Occupancy-coupled power control**: the DPC loop (§IV-B eqs. (27)–(31)) steps per-beam power up or down by observing per-beam EE, and eq. (30) forces the step positive whenever any user in the beam falls below `γ_thr`. Since the per-user SINR requirement rises with `U_n,m` through eq. (9), the loop is occupancy-driven. (iii) **Handover**: intra-LEO beam switching plus inter-LEO handover gated by a 3GPP-A3-style pair of conditions — SINR offset `γ_n*,m*,k − γ_os > γ_n,m,k` with `γ_os = 6 dB` (eq. (24)) and a time-to-trigger `T^trig ≥ T_thr` (eq. (25)) — Algorithm 2.
