/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-fable-ws/DECOMP-fable-2026-09-10.md:63:2. **Error accumulates quadratically.** Ψ is a set function over k(k−1)/2 pairs (plus material higher-order terms, `R3` median 1.32·|Ψ| in 20/20 panels). The measured mean signed error per pair at epoch 200 is +0.535 at k = 100, giving 0.535 × 4950 = **2,648** against an exact ΔF of 40. A per-*user* bias of 0.1 on an additive-over-users score tilts the same row by 0.1 × 100 = **10**. Same panel, factor 265 between the two.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-fable-ws/DECOMP-fable-2026-09-10.md:65:Adding to this, ABLATION-SURVIVAL and SELECTOR-DIAGNOSIS show the **learned singleton path alone** (Ψ̂ ≡ 0) already picks the size-100 row 10/10. So under S-cat the residual is not merely ill-conditioned; it is stacked on top of an additive path that is itself 4x off at k = 100 (learned 106.5 vs exact 480.1). Conditioning is necessary, not sufficient — which is why §5 declares learnability measurements rather than asserting success.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-fable-ws/DECOMP-fable-2026-09-10.md:86:- *S-cat (pilot, `ProfileSelector`):* T1, T2, T3 are all nonzero on every row including the ~700 singleton rows. Any one of them can flip the argmax between any pair of rows, including singleton-vs-singleton, which the residual can never do. The hard-zero anchor is not needed and is not used.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-fable-ws/DECOMP-fable-2026-09-10.md:87:- *Panel harness (S-move):* on a single-user move, T2 is the source chain's switch-off or the destination chain's activation plus the PA change; T1 is the mover's own bits plus the co-occupants' airtime/mode change. Both are nonzero on singletons and both are absent from the per-user `argmax(q1+q2)` seed (whose Q1 context is `b⁻₋ᵢ` before insertion). So an informative T1 or T2 changes the ranking of the seed's single-user neighbours and therefore the **first accepted move**; a neutral one leaves it to the other. Round-1 Theorem 1 no longer has a hypothesis to bite on.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-fable-ws/DECOMP-fable-2026-09-10.md:90:1. It is a **declared policy change** to §B4 (route identities), §C2 (score form, singleton anchor removed), §A1/§A2 (T1's context is candidate-state coordinator information). Rationale independent of any route's sign: the conditioning arithmetic above, computed from exact physics on TRAIN anchors before any EE contrast exists. It is a v2 contract, not an amendment or a bug fix.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-fable-ws/DECOMP-fable-2026-09-10.md:114:4. **Learnability from admissible information.** Held-out-by-anchor R² and within-row pairwise ordering of (i) a T1 head given the candidate-occupancy context and (ii) a T2 head given per-chain RF/activation vectors. Accept ordering ≥ 0.80 (the externality-free oracle reached 0.902; the sealed 16-D head 0.650); reject < 0.70. Below 0.70 on either, that route is not learnable from admissible inputs and the decomposition collapses to two routes.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-fable-ws/DECOMP-fable-2026-09-10.md:158:**Verified from code or from the numbers above.** The three selector paths and their move sets; hard-zero singleton Ψ̂ in the head itself; contexts built only for |A| ≥ 2 in the pilot; the proposal builder never consults `psi`; the r8 smoke's search is first-improvement with all learned arms sharing one seed hash; catalogue composition (base + all unilaterals + 2 S0 proposals + ≤ 180 pairs + evacuations, cap 1,500) and the 911/701 row counts; the §2 exact table and its identities (`Σd + Ψ = ΔF`, `ΔF = Δbits/κ − cost` to 1e-3); per-user bits summing to network bits; per-component energy summing to network joules; scalar `evaluate` ignoring `boundary_indices` while `evaluate_many` honours them; the spine decision fixing first-improvement for the panel; 5/10 committed rows changing when Ψ̂ is zeroed under S-cat.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-fable-ws/DECOMP-fable-2026-09-10.md:160:**Derived on paper.** The parts-to-total ratios (1.00–1.61x vs 1.57–22.99x); O(k) vs O(k²) bias accumulation (10 vs 2,648 at k = 100); the ≤ 1.72 bound on Σsd/sd for the term split and the positive-covariance inequality; the identity `Ψ = [T1(A) − ΣT1({i})] − η[T2(A) − ΣT2({i})]`; reachability of each term on singleton rows and single-user moves; that the multi-start headroom is a seed/order prize outside any head's control under the harness; that the two "unexplained" receipts are forced by the proposal builder and by Theorem 1 respectively.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-astra-ws/DECOMP-ASTRA-2026-09-10.md:3:My inclination is now constructive: the corrected selector removes the supposed structural barrier to C3. That is a substantial revision, not merely a qualification. I can give a precise information-based construction and a conditioning theorem, but cannot establish its operational assumptions or the required EE effects from the supplied evidence. Below I give the strongest opposing case before reaching that conclusion.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-strategic-astra-ws/DECOMP-ASTRA-2026-09-10.md:13:An additional code detail narrows one hypothesis: [pilot context construction](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-selector-ws/scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py:1510) creates a context for every catalogue configuration whose recorded changed set has size at least two. Thus context omission for empty/singleton rows is structural in this loop. Whether most actual rows fall into those categories, or another bridge omits contexts, still requires counts. I did not measure them.
