    91	## Decision
    92	
    93	**`PRODUCTION-GEOMETRY-BLOCKED`.** Gates 2, 3, and 7 pass; gate 4 fails.
    94	
    95	**Scope of the closure (binding).** This closes the pair
    96	`moving-footprint-lvlh-hex7-v1 + Walker 180/9/1 at 53°` as a *multi-satellite
    97	production contract*. It does not establish that moving footprints are
    98	unnecessary, that no constellation could support them, or that the existing
    99	steered-cell path is sufficient as a benchmark. Those are separate questions,
   100	and none of them is answered here.
   101	
   102	- **[grounded]** A fixed, non-retrospective service area does exist: the narrow
   103	  10×10 km patch on the pre-existing Family-B centre reaches full 100-UE
   104	  coverage in 528 slots per day, with up to 21 contiguous slots.
   105	- **[grounded]** Same-physical-link dwell reaches 10 consecutive slots, well
   106	  above the two-slot requirement, so the recurrence has room to run.
   107	- **[grounded]** The declared 200×90 km production service area has **zero**
   108	  full-coverage slots in 24 h. This independently reproduces the earlier
   109	  288-phase `STRUCTURAL-PASS-COVERAGE-BLOCKED` finding on a different sampling
   110	  grid.
   111	- **[grounded]** Multi-satellite handover opportunity is **absent everywhere**:
   112	  no slot in either scan had two satellites covering the area, and no UE ever
   113	  had a candidate from two distinct satellites.
   114	
   115	The failure is structural for this constellation, not statistical. The
   116	candidate rule admits off-axis angles up to the **full** HPBW, so the widest
   117	reachable edge is `ring_tilt + full HPBW = 5.32°` off-nadir, which reaches
   118	**72.67 km** from the sub-satellite point. Two satellites can therefore
   119	illuminate a common point only when their sub-satellite points are within
   120	about **145.35 km**. The producer sweeps Walker `180/9/1` at 1 s resolution
   121	over a full orbital period (`6071.7646 s`) and records the **global minimum**
   122	nearest-neighbour sub-satellite separation as **`342.1771 km`** at
   123	`t = 1636 s` — artifact keys `global_min_sub_satellite_spacing_km`,
   124	`global_min_at_time_s`, and `shared_candidate_margin_ratio = 2.3542`. That is
   125	the tightest the constellation ever gets, so no sampling density recovers a
   126	choice the geometry does not contain. The focused test asserts the producer's
   127	sweep agrees with an independent recomputation.
   128	
   129	(An earlier draft of this record quoted `484 / 912 / 1638 km` as the p05 / p50 /
   130	p95 spacing. Those are a `t = 0` snapshot across satellite pairs, not a
   131	statistic over the 24 h support, and they overstated the margin. The global
   132	minimum above supersedes them; the verdict is unchanged.)
   133	
   134	The pre-filter cannot manufacture this zero. A satellite can only reach a user
   135	in the service area if its sub-satellite point lies within reach plus the area
   136	half-extent. The probe computes that half-extent on the sphere from the actual
   137	user set — `109.9271 km` for the widest declared area — so the required radius
   138	is `72.6732 + 109.9271 = 182.6003 km`, against a `300 km` coarse sweep, a
   139	`117.3997 km` margin. The probe asserts this before scanning and fails closed
   140	if it is ever violated. (An earlier draft quoted the planar `109.66 / 182.33 km`
   141	figures; the artifact keys `service_area_half_extent_km` and
   142	`capture_radius_margin_km` are authoritative.)
   143	
   144	**Scope of "structural":** this is established for Walker `180/9/1` at `53°`,
   145	the constellation the production adapter already declares. It is a statement
   146	about the ratio of a `72.67 km` footprint reach to that constellation's
   147	spacing, not a proof about every conceivable constellation; option 2 below is
   148	exactly the route that would revisit it.
   149	
   150	Sequential turnover does not substitute: coverage windows are ~5 minutes apart
   151	(median gap `300 s` on the narrow patch), which is orders of magnitude longer
   152	than the 1 s slot and any episode horizon under consideration. Within an
   153	episode the satellite index is degenerate, so a per-user `(satellite, beam)`
   154	action space collapses to a single-satellite beam-selection problem.
   155	
   156	## What is explicitly NOT done
   157	
   158	Per the standing anti-repair rule, none of the following was applied, and none
   159	may be applied to convert this into a pass:
   160	
   161	- widening `half_power_full_width_deg`, `ring_tilt_deg`, or `g0_linear`;
   162	- adding a power multiplier, cap, clip, or projection;
   163	- raising `max_active_beams` above `v_max = k_cap = 3`;
   164	- substituting a denser or tighter constellation chosen after seeing this result;
   165	- re-centring the service area after seeing coverage statistics;
   166	- lowering the UE count below 100.
   167	
   168	Consequently **no environment adapter, calibration, preregistration, source
   169	fence, or episode-0 run is created for this track**. The downstream steps that
   170	depended on a frozen production contract are gated here.
   171	
   172	## Resolution of the three options
   173	
   174	The owner declined to choose and delegated the decision to a cross-model G6
   175	review. Codex ran an independent read-only adversarial review (transcript and
   176	brief: `.scratch/moving-footprint-codex-g6-20260821/`). The controller and the
   177	reviewer converged, with the reviewer tightening the scope in three places.
   178	
   179	**Adopted: Option 3 — keep the moving-footprint track as a diagnostic mechanism
   180	result and leave the active benchmark on the existing steered-cell Family-B
   181	path, returning the critical path to the active-contract preregistration and
   182	source fence.**
   183	
   184	This is a **scope and priority decision, not a refutation**:
   185	
   186	1. **Option 1 (single-satellite beam handover) is rejected as the active
   187	   source-faithful benchmark.** The source paper's handover is beam handover
   188	   across the beams of *multiple* satellites, and its Fig. 4 varies the number
   189	   of satellites and reports the weighted reward rising with it
   190	   (`paper-source/txt_layout/2024_09_Handover_…layout.txt:391`). That ablation
   191	   is only meaningful when several satellites offer candidates at once. A
   192	   geometry in which one satellite is ever a candidate cannot express it. This
   193	   rejects Option 1 as *the benchmark*; it does not forbid a separately framed
   194	   intra-satellite beam-handover study.
   195	2. **Option 2 (a different constellation) is deferred, not refuted.** The
   196	   earlier draft of this record called a `≤145.35 km` local pair "not a
   197	   realistic LEO constellation". The repository holds no engineering or
   198	   literature evidence for that claim, so it is withdrawn. Option 2 stays open
   199	   and requires an *external* constellation justification plus a prospective
   200	   freeze before it is probed — a pre-declared design input is not the same as
   201	   retrospectively repairing this failed contract.
   202	3. **Option 3 is adopted on scope and priority**, not on proof that the moving
   203	   footprint is unnecessary. See the adjudication of finding H-1 below.
   204	
   205	Follow-on requirement carried into the Family-B line: the new preregistration
   206	should declare an **angle-leverage sufficiency check under the actual learned
   207	and evaluation policies**, because the evidence below is an existence result
   208	under hand-declared policies only.
   209	
   210	## Cross-model review adjudication (codex G6, 2026-08-21)
   211	
   212	Every finding was re-derived against the files before acceptance. Two earlier
   213	findings (the `3.66°` beam-edge constant and the `t=0` spacing snapshot) were
   214	accepted and fixed before this round and are recorded inline above.
   215	
   216	| # | Severity | Finding | Adjudication |
   217	|---|---|---|---|
   218	| H-1 | HIGH | The evidence standard is asymmetric: the moving track must clear eight production gates, while the existing path earns `ANGLE-LIVE` as soon as one policy leaves a `±1e-3` band — and that witness is the adversarial `maximum-theta` policy. So it shows angle leverage *exists*; it does not show the track is *not needed*. | **Accepted in full.** Verified: `minimum-theta` sits at p05 `0.9995` / p95 `1.0004`, inside the band, so the verdict rests entirely on `maximum-theta`. The design-doc heading claiming the track "is not needed for r1" contradicted its own body and has been retitled; controller reasoning (c) is downgraded from "removes the motivation" to "removes the *premise that the recurrence is inert*". |
   219	| M-1 | MEDIUM | Dwell and coverage were measured on the pre-admission nearest candidate; `MovingFootprintActionCatalog.admit` was never called, so a candidate winner was reported as a served physical link. | **Accepted.** The probe now applies the three-beam admission and reports candidate and served views separately; the contract gates are judged on the served view. Measured effect: admission does not bind on either service area (`max_active_beams_on_any_satellite = 3`, no user dropped), so the numbers coincide — but the earlier keys were misnamed and are corrected. |
   220	| M-2 | MEDIUM | Reasoning (b) exceeded the tested surface: nothing in the repository supports "no realistic constellation permits a `≤145.35 km` pair". | **Accepted.** Claim withdrawn; Option 2 is now recorded as deferred, not refuted. |
   221	| M-3 | MEDIUM | The session provides no auditable data-blind freeze: every script was written this session and the thresholds were chosen with smoke results already visible. | **Accepted, with a distinction.** All three artifacts are labelled diagnostics, not data-blind evidence. Note that the blocking result is **threshold-free** — a count of exactly zero plus a `2.35×` geometric margin — whereas `ANGLE-LIVE` *does* depend on a threshold chosen after seeing smoke output. That asymmetry is now disclosed at both sites. |
   222	| L-1 | LOW | The "24 h" refined grid was not clipped to its support (36 slots ran to `86435`), and dwell/contiguity credited continuity across non-adjacent rows. | **Accepted.** Slots are clipped to `[0, support)`, and dwell, contiguous coverage, and coverage windows now require a genuine one-slot step. |
   223	| L-2 | LOW | Calling the 100-UE audit "independent" is too strong — same extent, same corners, same patch builder. | **Accepted.** Wording changed to "separate"; the design record already states that coverage is inherited and that the new content is the runtime-law verification. |
   224	| L-3 | LOW | Round-2 cleanup was incomplete: a stale `484 km` reference, an "about 50 km" test docstring, planar rather than spherical half-extent figures, and a `1 s` spacing sweep claimed but not preserved by any artifact or test. | **Accepted in full.** All four corrected; the producer now computes the global spacing minimum itself and records it in the artifact, and the focused test asserts the producer's sweep agrees with an independent one. |
   225	
   226	Reviewer items not accepted as defects: none. The reviewer's own sampling
   227	attacks (coarse step, pad, pre-filter, inter-slot motion) failed to refute the
   228	blocker, and its independent `1 s` full-period recomputation of `342.1771 km`
